    {
      "text": "In 1932, Australia went to war with birds. About 20,000 emus were flattening the wheat fields of Western Australia, and desperate farmers, many of them war veterans, asked for machine guns. So the army sent two soldiers, two Lewis guns, and 10,000 rounds. The plan was simple. The emus had a different one. They split into small groups and scattered across the countryside. The soldiers fired. The emus kept running. After five weeks, the army reported 986 emus down and the wheat still being eaten. The army went home. The emus did not.",
      "start_time": 0.14,
      "end_time": 34.86,
